              ]
            },
            "title": "Synthesis"
          },
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">Dear colleague, I am writing to inform you about the hospital course of your patient, a 63-year-old male, admitted on 08/05/1997 with acute right lower quadrant abdominal pain, nausea, and low-grade fever, clinically and biochemically consistent with acute appendicitis. On admission, physical examination revealed marked tenderness at McBurney's point with guarding. Blood tests confirmed leukocytosis with a left shift. Abdominal ultrasound supported the diagnosis. The patient underwent laparoscopic appendectomy on 09/05/1997 without intraoperative complications. Histopathology confirmed acute appendicitis. Postoperatively, he received intravenous antibiotics and analgesics, with adequate wound care. Recovery was uneventful, and the patient was mobilized early with physiotherapy support. He was discharged home on 10/05/1997 in stable condition with wound care instructions and follow-up recommendations. Please ensure outpatient wound review within one week.</div>"
            },
            "code": {
